WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.943
(slow mellow music)

00:00:00.943 --> 00:00:02.888
- [Voiceover] This is the Speke’s gazelle.

00:00:02.888 --> 00:00:06.232
The smallest of the gazelle species.

00:00:06.232 --> 00:00:09.982
Sadly, it is also one
of the most endangered.

00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:14.835
They max out at around
two feet and 40 pounds.

00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:16.820
Their biggest feature is their nose

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.200
which can puff up to the
size of a tennis ball

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.733
when excited.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.463
Native to the Horn of Africa,

00:00:22.463 --> 00:00:24.843
they live in semidesert grasslands

00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:29.553
and feed on grass, herbs,
shrubs and other plants.

00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:30.494
In the 80's,

00:00:30.494 --> 00:00:32.920
they were one of the most
abundant gazelle species

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.892
in parts of Ethiopia and Somalia,

00:00:35.892 --> 00:00:40.606
but today the Ethiopian
population is close to extinction

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:43.714
while numbers in Somalia
have greatly decreased.

00:00:43.714 --> 00:00:46.965
A combination of hunting,
drought and overgrazing

00:00:46.965 --> 00:00:49.382
has decimated the population.

00:00:51.549 --> 00:00:53.799
This is the Speke’s gazelle.

00:00:58.472 --> 00:01:01.472
(slow mellow music)

